From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: feat: CustomFrameCap -- exact FPS cap via swap-ack pacing, with runtime adjustment

Chromium-side half of the frame cap. Apply from the Chromium src root.
The Electron-shell half is frame-cap-electron-patch.diff.

Under --disable-frame-rate-limit the per-window BeginFrame source is
BackToBackBeginFrameSource, whose cadence is gated only by swap-buffer acks
through DisplayScheduler's SetIsGpuBusy latch. Stretching those ack releases
onto a fixed interval yields an exact cap with dense submission, avoiding the
vsync-grid snapping that broke earlier metadata-based attempts.

DidReceiveSwapBuffersAck() becomes a pacing gate; its stock body moves verbatim
into DidReceiveSwapBuffersAckImpl(). Core invariant: every gate-deferred ack
owes exactly one Impl() call. pending_release_count_ is that ledger and a single
base::DeadlineTimer (kPrecise, absolute deadline -- PostDelayedTaskAt's PassKey
is not available to viz) only schedules the next payout. There are no per-ack
structures and never more than one timer. Releases are FIFO along the grid.

The grid advances by whole intervals and is never re-anchored to Now() on the
immediate-release path: re-anchoring makes the cap a per-frame floor where a
cycle can overshoot but never compensate, landing the mean measurably under
target (measured on an earlier revision: a stable but wrong 229.9 at a 240
target, vs 238.1 with the grid).

Display::Resize and Display::DisableSwapUntilResize flush the pending delay,
the latter before ForceImmediateSwapIfPossible() -- AttemptDrawAndSwap() gates
on pending_swaps_ < MaxPendingSwaps(), so a held ack would otherwise make the
forced pre-resize swap a silent no-op. FlushPendingSwapAckDelay() and
SetFrameCapInterval() are no-op virtuals on DisplaySchedulerBase because
Display holds the scheduler as a base pointer.

Runtime adjustment adds DisplayPrivate.SetFrameCapInterval(TimeDelta), a
dedicated mojo method rather than a reuse of the vsync-parameter channel so
that real display vsync updates can never be interpreted as cap commands.
RootCompositorFrameSinkImpl forwards it to Display (already on the viz thread,
so a direct call). Compositor caches the value and re-sends it wherever the
other display properties are re-sent on frame-sink re-bind, so a hidden->shown
window keeps its cap. Unlike SetDisplayVSyncParameters, Compositor::
SetFrameCapInterval deliberately has no kDisableFrameRateLimit early-out: that
switch is precisely the mode the cap exists for.

Semantics: no flag -> 0 -> stock behavior on every path;
--enable-features=CustomFrameCap:fps/240 -> capped at 240. Clamped to 30..1000.

KNOWN LIMITATION -- do not ship without reading:
The cap holds only at renderer submit-queue depth 1. Co-emitting
CustomMaxPendingFrames:count/N with N>=2 does not merely disable the cap, it
inverts it: measured rAF rates rise as the cap tightens (at count/2 on a box
whose depth-2 uncapped ceiling is 551 fps: cap 1000 -> 535, 285 -> 555,
240 -> 666, 120 -> 804, 60 -> 1008, 30 -> 1381). The two features are in
direct tension by design -- CustomMaxPendingFrames exists to let the renderer
run ahead of the draw loop, and this cap works by pacing that same draw loop.
The decoupling happens in cc's scheduler in the renderer process, upstream of
anything DisplayScheduler can influence, so no change confined to viz fixes it.
Either cap the renderer submit gate as well, or do not co-emit the two features.

PORTED to Chromium 152.0.7977 (Electron 44) on 2026-08-25. One behavioural port
decision plus three context re-anchors:

  * Display::DisableSwapUntilResize() was DELETED upstream (present in
    151.0.7900, gone by 152.0.7938) along with swapped_since_resize_. Its
    FlushPendingSwapAckDelay() call moves to
    Display::ForceImmediateDrawAndSwapIfPossible(), now the only caller of
    scheduler_->ForceImmediateSwapIfPossible(). The flush still runs BEFORE the
    forced swap, for the same reason: AttemptDrawAndSwap() gates on
    pending_swaps_ < MaxPendingSwaps(args), so a held ack silently no-ops it.
  * class DisplayScheduler::BeginFrameObserver moved from the .cc into the
    header's protected section.
  * The ctor init list and the member block gained
    allow_multiple_swaps_per_vsync_ / use_platform_preferred_deadlines_ exactly
    where cap_interval_ was inserted. The frame-cap members are now declared
    AFTER both of those so member-init order still matches declaration order.
  * MaxPendingSwaps() split into MaxPendingSwapsForRefreshRate /
    MaxPendingSwapsForDeadline / MaxPendingSwaps(const BeginFrameArgs&), and
    current_frame_display_time() gained an args parameter.

